# Generators (glslc.exe from shader-tools/windows-x86_64 of the named NDK):
#   filming, printing, scan_spectral_chain: Android NDK 27.0.12077973
#     (reported tool shaderc v2022.3 ndk-r26c; target SPIR-V 1.0). These three
#     are the qualified pointwise chain of #148 and are NOT regenerated with the
#     current NDK: a newer glslc emits different bytes for the same source, and
#     the device evidence for that slice is bound to these exact binaries.
#   halation_scatter: Android NDK 28.2.13676358, the pinned build NDK (#206).
#   grain: Android NDK 28.2.13676358, the pinned build NDK (#214).
#   glare: Android NDK 28.2.13676358, the pinned build NDK (#215).
#   fft_convolve: Android NDK 28.2.13676358, the pinned build NDK (#216).
#   filming_stage: Android NDK 28.2.13676358, the pinned build NDK (#218).
#   contrast_mask: Android NDK 28.2.13676358, the pinned build NDK.
#     It duplicates filming.comp's math ON PURPOSE, split at the log10 so
#     the spatial stages can run between the halves; filming.comp keeps its
#     NDK 27 bytes because the #148 device evidence is bound to them.
# Digests are over LF content, which is what git stores; a Windows working tree
# checks these files out with CRLF, so normalise before comparing.
# Exact command (run once for each source/output pair from this directory):
# glslc.exe -fshader-stage=compute --target-env=vulkan1.1 -mfmt=c -o OUTPUT.inc SOURCE.comp
